{%- comment -%}
  sections/main-product-lada.liquid
  Rendered by templates/product.lada.json, which puts .template-product-lada
  on <body>. Every rule in the CSS block that dresses this file is scoped to
  that class, so the dark register ends the moment the customer leaves Lada.

  WHY THIS EXISTS AND IS NOT A RESTYLE OF main-product.liquid.
  The shared PDP sells other people's cloth: it carries the white-label
  vendor rule, a KJC SKU line, a percent-off chip, a five-row metafield
  spec table, a trust list and a related-products grid. That is nine
  blocks of text before the description. The client rejected exactly that
  density on the Lada page. Lada is the house's own label, everything in
  it is cotton, and the reference the client gave (faizasaqlain.pk) puts
  one word on a page and lets the photographs do the rest. So this file
  carries six things and refuses the rest:

      title, price, variant choice, quantity, add to bag, description

  Nothing is invented around the description. It prints exactly as the
  client typed it in Shopify, with no heading over it and no sentence
  underneath.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, so nobody adds it back by accident:
  breadcrumb trail (one back link instead, and only when the customer
  arrived from a listing), SKU, vendor, spec table, trust list, percent
  off chip, related products grid. Each of those is a paragraph the page
  has to earn and none of them earns it.

  COMMERCE IS NOT REBUILT. The form is the same contract kj-cart.js
  already listens for, document-wide and class-independent:
      form[data-kj-atc]  +  [data-kj-atc-error]  +  a submit button
  so add to bag, the error string Shopify sends back, the drawer and the
  count all behave exactly as they do on the shared PDP.

  WHY THE MARKUP DOES NOT REUSE .pdp__form / .pdp__buy / .pdp__gal.
  kj-v2.js binds those three classes at parse time. Its option handler
  reads `.value` off every [data-pdp-option] element, which is correct
  for a <select> and wrong for a radio (a radio reports its own value
  whether or not it is checked), and its quantity stepper clamps a
  minimum and no maximum. This page needs radios, because a colour has
  to read as a colour, and a cap of three, because Lada is made in small
  numbers. Borrowing those class names would double-bind and break both.
  So the markup uses its own lpdp__ names and the small script at the
  bottom of this file, and kj-v2.js is left untouched.

  TWO KINDS OF PRODUCT SHARE THIS TEMPLATE, detected by option name and
  never by handle, because /collections/alamgeer does not exist yet and a
  hardcoded handle would be wrong the day it does:

    Alamgeer       100% combed cotton. One option, Colour, no size. The
                   colour values render as swatches when the client has
                   set swatch values in Shopify, and as plain named
                   buttons when he has not, which is the state this ships
                   in. Under the choice sits ONE line and a WhatsApp
                   button about being measured. Both are settings and
                   both disappear when cleared.

    Ready to wear  stitched waistcoats. One option, Size, S / M / L.
                   Plain buttons, with the size guide beside the label.

  A product with neither, or with no options at all, simply gets neither
  the measure line nor the guide and still buys correctly.

  PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT, the same standard as the rest of this theme.
  A one-option product (which is both kinds above) posts the variant id
  straight off the radio, so choosing a colour and buying it works with
  the script blocked. The script only upgrades: it swaps the price when
  a shop prices sizes differently, flips the button to Sold out, caps the
  quantity, and reveals the plus and minus buttons, which ship hidden
  because a control that does nothing is worse than no control.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign v = product.selected_or_first_available_variant | default: product.variants.first

  assign add_label   = section.settings.add_label   | default: 'Add to bag'
  assign sold_label  = section.settings.sold_label  | default: 'Sold out'
  assign unav_label  = section.settings.unav_label  | default: 'Unavailable'
  assign guide_label = section.settings.guide_label | default: 'Size guide'
  assign qty_max     = section.settings.qty_max     | default: 3
  assign qty_label   = section.settings.qty_label   | default: 'Quantity'

  assign wa      = section.settings.whatsapp
  assign wa_text = section.settings.whatsapp_text | default: 'Hello Khan Jee Center. I would like to be measured for'
  assign measure_line = section.settings.measure_line
  assign measure_cta  = section.settings.measure_cta

  comment
    The button needs BOTH a label and a number. Without this the client
    clearing only the number leaves the measure line rendering an empty
    paragraph, which on this page is a stray 16px of nothing under the
    colours.
  endcomment
  assign measure_wa = false
  if measure_cta != blank and wa != blank
    assign measure_wa = true
  endif

  comment
    Which option is a colour and which is a size, decided by the option's
    own name against two editable lists. handleize on both sides so
    "Colour", "colour" and "COLOUR" are one thing, and so the client can
    add "shade" or "rang" to the list without a developer.
  endcomment
  assign colour_keys = section.settings.colour_options | default: 'colour,color' | split: ','
  assign size_keys   = section.settings.size_options   | default: 'size' | split: ','

  assign has_colour = false
  assign has_size = false
  for opt in product.options
    assign key = opt | handleize
    for c in colour_keys
      assign ck = c | handleize
      if ck == key
        assign has_colour = true
      endif
    endfor
    for s in size_keys
      assign sk = s | handleize
      if sk == key
        assign has_size = true
      endif
    endfor
  endfor

  comment
    The measure line belongs to Alamgeer, the 100% combed cotton, and to
    nothing else. A product that carries a size is already cut, so the
    answer to "will it fit" is the size guide, not the tailor.
  endcomment
  assign show_measure = false
  if has_colour and has_size == false
    assign show_measure = true
  endif

  assign has_options = false
  unless product.has_only_default_variant
    assign has_options = true
  endunless

  comment
    ONE OPTION IS THE NO-SCRIPT PATH. With a single option each value maps
    to exactly one variant, so the radio can simply BE the variant id and
    the form posts the right thing with nothing running. product_option_value.variant
    is nil on a multi-option product and on any shop still serving the old
    string-array option values, so it is probed rather than assumed; when
    it comes back empty the page falls through to the combination path
    below, which is the behaviour the shared PDP already has.
  endcomment
  assign direct_ids = false
  if has_options and product.options.size == 1
    assign probe = product.options_with_values[0].values | first
    if probe.variant.id != blank
      assign direct_ids = true
    endif
  endif

  comment
    Only build the per-variant price spans when prices actually differ.
    Every price on this page is rendered by Liquid's money filter and
    swapped by toggling `hidden`; no script here ever formats a currency,
    which is the same rule kj-cart.js states at the top of its own file.
  endcomment
  assign price_varies = product.price_varies
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  THE TITLE, TRIMMED THE SAME WAY EVERY OTHER PAGE TRIMS IT.

  sections/main-product.liquid, snippets/product-card.liquid and
  snippets/lada-product-card.liquid all print
  `product.title | split: ' - ' | first`, because Shopify titles on this
  store carry a " - suffix" the shop uses for its own filing. This page
  printed the raw title, so the same piece was called one thing in the
  grid and another at the top of its own page. `split` on a title with
  no " - " in it returns the whole title, so a clean name is untouched.

  The WhatsApp message carries the trimmed name AND the page address,
  same as the shared product page. The shopkeeper used to receive a
  sentence with a product name in it and no way to open the piece it
  was about.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign pdp_title = product.title | split: ' - ' | first -%}
{%- capture wa_msg -%}{{ wa_text }} {{ pdp_title }}. {{ shop.url }}{{ product.url }}{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The three button strings live on the root as attributes rather than
  only inside the script below. A theme-editor reload hands the page a
  NEW root with the client's newly saved wording already rendered into
  these, and the re-init at the bottom reads them from there, so it can
  never repaint the button with the wording this script was printed
  with. See the note above that script.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="lada lpdp" id="lpdp-{{ section.id }}" data-lpdp
     data-lpdp-add="{{ add_label | escape }}"
     data-lpdp-sold="{{ sold_label | escape }}"
     data-lpdp-unav="{{ unav_label | escape }}">
  <div class="lada__wrap lpdp__wrap">

    {%- comment -%}
      One back link, not a breadcrumb trail, and only when the customer
      came in through a listing. It is navigation rather than writing:
      eleven pixels, one word, and it is the way back to the wall of
      photographs he was looking at. With no collection in the URL it is
      not rendered at all rather than falling back to "Home".
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if collection -%}
      <a class="lpdp__back" href="{{ collection.url }}">{{ collection.title }}</a>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="lpdp__grid">

      {%- comment -%}
        ---------- the contact sheet ----------
        Every frame at once, 4:5, two up, eight pixels apart. No
        thumbnail rail and no slideshow: a rail is a second copy of the
        same pictures that costs a tab stop each, and a slideshow hides
        the photographs behind a control, which on a page whose entire
        argument is the photographs is the one thing it must not do.

        4:5 is .pcard__media's ratio site-wide. The client asked for that
        uniformity explicitly.

        A single photograph spans both columns instead of sitting at half
        width beside a hole. On a phone the sheet is one column, because
        a 4:5 frame at 170px wide is a thumbnail, and thumbnails are what
        this layout exists to avoid.
      {%- endcomment -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        WHY `sizes` IS SPELLED OUT IN PIXELS AND NOT IN vw, and why a lone
        photograph gets its own string. This was measured in a browser,
        not estimated, because an earlier pass estimated it and was wrong
        in two places at once.

        .lada__wrap is min(100% - 2.5rem, 1180px), and min(100% - 4rem,
        1180px) from 900px up. The sheet is the whole wrap below 1000px,
        and the wrap minus the 380px details column and the --s8 (72px)
        gutter above it. No vw fraction describes that, and the 30vw /
        44vw guess that used to sit here missed twice:

          960px  frame renders at 444px, was declared 288px
          1280px a LONE photograph spans both columns and renders at
                 728px, was declared 360px

        Both of those fetch an image about half the width they draw at
        and upscale it. On a page whose entire argument is the
        photographs that is the one thing it must not do, and a lone
        photograph is the state most products ship in while the client
        is still shooting them.

        The breakpoints are the wrap's own, not round numbers: 1244px is
        where 100vw - 4rem stops being smaller than the 1180px cap, and
        900px is where the side gutter goes from 2.5rem to 4rem.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- liquid
        if product.media.size == 1
          assign sheet_sizes = '(min-width:1244px) 728px, (min-width:1000px) calc(100vw - 516px), (min-width:900px) calc(100vw - 64px), calc(100vw - 40px)'
        else
          assign sheet_sizes = '(min-width:1244px) 360px, (min-width:1000px) calc((100vw - 524px) / 2), (min-width:900px) calc((100vw - 72px) / 2), (min-width:700px) calc((100vw - 48px) / 2), calc(100vw - 40px)'
        endif
      -%}

      <div class="lpdp__sheet{% if product.media.size == 1 %} lpdp__sheet--one{% endif %}">
        {%- if product.media.size > 0 -%}
          {%- for media in product.media -%}
            {%- liquid
              assign mw = media.width | default: 1400
              assign src_w = 900
              if mw < src_w
                assign src_w = mw
              endif
            -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              data-reveal is the site's own scroll-reveal hook, the one
              sections/category-grid.liquid and store-location.liquid
              already use, so the Lada journey animates the same way the
              white shop does rather than growing an effect of its own.

              NEVER ON THE FIRST FRAME. It is the page's largest paint
              and it is above the fold on every screen; site.js hides
              every [data-reveal] the moment it adds .js-reveal, so a
              reveal there is a blank plate where the photograph should
              be for as long as it takes the observer to call back. Every
              frame after it is genuinely below the fold on a phone and
              on a laptop both.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <figure class="lpdp__frame"
              {%- unless forloop.first %} data-reveal style="--d:{{ forloop.index0 | minus: 1 | times: 70 | at_most: 350 }}ms"{% endunless %}>
              {%- comment -%}
                The 1600w step is here for the lone photograph: it draws
                at 728px, which a retina screen wants 1456px for, and
                without it the candidate list jumps from 1100 straight to
                whatever the client uploaded, so a 4000px camera file is
                pulled down to fill a 1456px need.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <img class="lpdp__img"
                   src="{{ media | image_url: width: src_w }}"
                   srcset="{%- if mw > 400 %}{{ media | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w, {% endif -%}
                           {%- if mw > 700 %}{{ media | image_url: width: 700 }} 700w, {% endif -%}
                           {%- if mw > 1100 %}{{ media | image_url: width: 1100 }} 1100w, {% endif -%}
                           {%- if mw > 1600 %}{{ media | image_url: width: 1600 }} 1600w, {% endif -%}
                           {{ media | image_url: width: mw }} {{ mw }}w"
                   sizes="{{ sheet_sizes }}"
                   width="{{ media.width }}" height="{{ media.height }}"
                   {% if forloop.first %}fetchpriority="high"{% else %}loading="lazy"{% endif %}
                   decoding="async"
                   alt="{{ media.alt | default: pdp_title | escape }}">
            </figure>
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The client loads every photograph himself after handover, so
            an unshot piece is a state this page will really ship in. An
            empty plate at --lada-panel against the --lada-ground page
            reads as a frame waiting for a picture; a collapsed column
            reads as a broken template. Same reasoning as the card.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="lpdp__frame lpdp__frame--empty" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      {%- comment -%} ---------- the details column ---------- {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="lpdp__info">
        <div class="lpdp__stick">

          <h1 class="lpdp__title">{{ pdp_title }}</h1>

          {%- comment -%}
            Price. Compare-at is kept, because the shared PDP has it and a
            template that silently drops a strikethrough would misprice a
            sale the day the client runs one. The percent-off chip is not
            kept: a coloured chip is the first thing that turns a house
            label back into a sale rack, which is the same call the Lada
            card makes.

            When the variants are priced differently every price is
            rendered here by Liquid and all but one carries `hidden`; the
            script swaps which one shows. Nothing below formats money.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <p class="lpdp__price">
            {%- if price_varies -%}
              {%- for variant in product.variants -%}
                <span class="lpdp__price-set" data-lpdp-vprice="{{ variant.id }}"
                      {% unless variant.id == v.id %}hidden{% endunless %}>
                  {%- if variant.compare_at_price > variant.price -%}
                    <s>{{ variant.compare_at_price | money }}</s>
                  {%- endif -%}
                  <span class="lpdp__now">{{ variant.price | money }}</span>
                </span>
              {%- endfor -%}
            {%- else -%}
              {%- if v.compare_at_price > v.price -%}
                <s>{{ v.compare_at_price | money }}</s>
              {%- endif -%}
              <span class="lpdp__now">{{ v.price | money }}</span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </p>

          <form action="{{ routes.cart_add_url }}" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data"
                class="lpdp__form" data-kj-atc>

            {%- if has_options -%}
              {%- for option in product.options_with_values -%}
                {%- liquid
                  assign okey = option.name | handleize

                  assign is_colour = false
                  for c in colour_keys
                    assign ck = c | handleize
                    if ck == okey
                      assign is_colour = true
                    endif
                  endfor

                  assign is_size = false
                  for s in size_keys
                    assign sk = s | handleize
                    if sk == okey
                      assign is_size = true
                    endif
                  endfor

                  comment
                    Swatches only when there is something to paint. The
                    client has not set a single swatch value yet, and a
                    row of identical grey discs is a worse colour picker
                    than a row of named buttons, so the dot is added per
                    option only if at least one value in it carries a
                    colour or an image.
                  endcomment
                  assign paint = false
                  if is_colour
                    for value in option.values
                      if value.swatch.color or value.swatch.image
                        assign paint = true
                      endif
                    endfor
                  endif
                -%}

                <div class="lpdp__opt{% if paint %} lpdp__opt--sw{% endif %}">
                  <div class="lpdp__opt-head">
                    <span class="lpdp__opt-l" id="{{ section.id }}-opt-{{ forloop.index0 }}">{{ option.name }}</span>

                    {%- comment -%}
                      The size guide sits beside the label it answers,
                      which is where the doubt actually happens, and it is
                      a <details> rather than a dialog or a [popover] on
                      purpose: it opens with no script at all. CSS lifts
                      the open panel off the flow on a wide screen so it
                      behaves as a popover; on a phone it expands in place,
                      which is the better shape there anyway. The script
                      only adds Escape and click-outside.

                      The table itself is snippets/lada-size-table, the
                      same component the Lada size section renders, so
                      there is one chart on this site and not two that
                      drift. uid keeps the two instances' unit toggles
                      from driving each other.

                      The chart's inches and centimetres radios therefore
                      sit inside the buy form and ride along in the POST
                      as lpdp-size-unit=in. /cart/add ignores every field
                      it does not know (only id, quantity, selling_plan
                      and properties[...] mean anything to it), so this is
                      inert rather than wrong. The alternative is lifting
                      the guide out of the form, which separates it from
                      the label it answers, and sitting beside that label
                      is the entire reason it is here.
                    {%- endcomment -%}
                    {%- if is_size -%}
                      {%- comment -%}
                        The uid carries the option index. Two size named
                        options on one product is not a thing anyone would
                        build, but if it ever happened the two charts would
                        share a radio name and one toggle would drive the
                        other, which is the exact failure the snippet's own
                        header warns about.
                      {%- endcomment -%}
                      {%- assign guide_uid = 'lpdp-size-' | append: forloop.index0 -%}
                      <details class="lpdp__guide" data-lpdp-guide>
                        <summary class="lpdp__guide-t">{{ guide_label }}</summary>
                        <div class="lpdp__guide-p">
                          {%- render 'lada-size-table',
                              uid: guide_uid,
                              rows: section.settings.size_rows,
                              columns: section.settings.size_columns,
                              note: section.settings.size_note -%}
                        </div>
                      </details>
                    {%- endif -%}
                  </div>

                  <div class="lpdp__values" role="radiogroup"
                       aria-labelledby="{{ section.id }}-opt-{{ forloop.index0 }}"
                       data-lpdp-group>
                    {%- assign oi = forloop.index0 -%}
                    {%- for value in option.values -%}
                      {%- liquid
                        assign uid = section.id | append: '-o' | append: oi | append: '-v' | append: forloop.index0

                        comment
                          Direct path: the radio carries the variant id and
                          an unavailable one is disabled, so a customer
                          with no script cannot pick a sold-out colour.
                          The one exception is spelled out below.
                          Combination path: the radio carries the option
                          value and availability is a hint only, because
                          whether a pairing exists is the script's job in
                          that case.
                        endcomment
                        assign r_name = 'id'
                        assign r_value = value.variant.id
                        assign r_checked = false
                        if value.variant.id == v.id
                          assign r_checked = true
                        endif
                        unless direct_ids
                          assign r_name = 'lpdp-o' | append: oi
                          assign r_value = value
                          assign r_checked = false
                          if option.selected_value == value or value.selected
                            assign r_checked = true
                          endif
                        endunless

                        assign r_avail = true
                        if value.available == false
                          assign r_avail = false
                        endif

                        comment
                          NEVER DISABLE THE CHECKED RADIO. On the direct
                          path the radio IS the id field, and a disabled
                          field is left out of the submission entirely, so
                          disabling the checked one posts a form with no
                          id at all and Shopify answers with an error page
                          rather than "sold out".

                          It happens for real: selected_or_first_available_variant
                          honours ?variant= in the URL even when that
                          variant is gone, so a shared link to a colour
                          that has since sold out lands here with the
                          sold-out radio checked while the product is
                          still available in other colours, which leaves
                          the button live. With the script running it
                          flips to Sold out a moment later; with the
                          script blocked the customer posts the sold-out
                          id and reads Shopify's own sold-out message,
                          which is the truth rather than a broken form.
                          It is still struck through and still says
                          is-out, so nothing about it looks buyable.
                        endcomment
                        assign r_disabled = false
                        if direct_ids and r_avail == false and r_checked == false
                          assign r_disabled = true
                        endif
                      -%}
                      <label class="lpdp__chip{% unless r_avail %} is-out{% endunless %}" for="{{ uid }}">
                        <input class="vh" type="radio" id="{{ uid }}"
                               name="{{ r_name }}" value="{{ r_value | escape }}"
                               {% if direct_ids %}data-lpdp-variant-radio{% endif %}
                               {% if r_checked %}checked{% endif %}
                               {% if r_disabled %}disabled{% endif %}>
                        {%- if paint -%}
                          {%- liquid
                            assign dot = ''
                            if value.swatch.image
                              assign dot_url = value.swatch.image | image_url: width: 80
                              assign dot = 'url(' | append: dot_url | append: ')'
                            elsif value.swatch.color
                              assign dot = 'rgb(' | append: value.swatch.color.rgb | append: ')'
                            endif
                          -%}
                          <span class="lpdp__dot" aria-hidden="true"
                                {% if dot != blank %}style="--dot:{{ dot | escape }}"{% endif %}></span>
                        {%- endif -%}
                        <span class="lpdp__chip-t">{{ value }}</span>
                        {%- comment -%}
                          A SOLD-OUT SIZE OR COLOUR HAS TO SAY SO IN
                          WORDS. Before this, the only thing marking one
                          was .is-out, which the stylesheet draws as
                          dimmed text with a line through it. A line
                          through a word is not in the accessibility
                          tree, so a screen reader read "Red, radio
                          button" for a colour that cannot be bought,
                          and on the combination path the radio is not
                          even disabled, so the customer can select it
                          and only learn the truth from the button
                          changing underneath him.

                          It rides inside the <label>, so it becomes
                          part of the radio's accessible name: "Red,
                          sold out". Not an aria-label, which would
                          replace the visible word and break voice
                          control for anyone who says "click red", and
                          not aria-disabled, which on the direct path
                          would contradict the real `disabled` attribute
                          and on the combination path would claim the
                          value is unusable when picking it is exactly
                          how the customer finds the pairing that works.

                          sold_label is the client's own string, the
                          same one the button and the quick view panel
                          use, so the three cannot drift.
                        {%- endcomment -%}
                        {%- unless r_avail -%}
                          <span class="vh">, {{ sold_label }}</span>
                        {%- endunless -%}
                      </label>
                    {%- endfor -%}
                  </div>
                </div>

                {%- comment -%}
                  ONE LINE, not a section. It is rendered under the colour
                  choice because that is the moment the question "but will
                  it fit me" arrives, and it is two settings so the client
                  owns both the sentence and the button. Clear either one
                  in the editor and that half stops rendering.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- if show_measure and is_colour -%}
                  {%- if measure_line != blank or measure_wa -%}
                    <p class="lpdp__measure">
                      {%- if measure_line != blank -%}
                        <span class="lpdp__measure-t">{{ measure_line }}</span>
                      {%- endif -%}
                      {%- if measure_wa -%}
                        <a class="lpdp__wa" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                           href="https://wa.me/{{ wa }}?text={{ wa_msg | strip | url_encode }}">
                          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                            <path d="M12 2a10 10 0 0 0-8.7 15L2 22l5.2-1.3A10 10 0 1 0 12 2Zm5.3 14.1c-.2.6-1.3 1.2-1.8 1.2-.5 0-1.1 0-1.9-.3a11 11 0 0 1-5.6-4.9c-.4-.7-.6-1.4-.6-2 0-.6.3-1.2.7-1.6.2-.2.4-.3.6-.3h.5c.2 0 .4 0 .5.4l.7 1.7c0 .2 0 .3-.1.5l-.4.5c-.1.2-.2.3 0 .5a7.8 7.8 0 0 0 3.4 2.9c.2.1.4 0 .5 0l.7-.8c.2-.2.3-.1.5 0l1.6.8c.2.1.3.2.3.3v.8Z"/>
                          </svg>
                          {{ measure_cta }}
                        </a>
                      {%- endif -%}
                    </p>
                  {%- endif -%}
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}

              {%- comment -%}
                The combination path needs a hidden id and the variant map.
                The direct path needs neither: the checked radio IS the id
                field. The map is still emitted for it, because the script
                reads a variant's availability and price out of it.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- unless direct_ids -%}
                <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ v.id }}" data-lpdp-variant-id>
              {%- endunless -%}

              <script type="application/json" data-lpdp-variants>
                [
                  {%- for variant in product.variants -%}
                    {"id":{{ variant.id }},
                     "options":{{ variant.options | json }},
                     "available":{{ variant.available }}}{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
                  {%- endfor -%}
                ]
              </script>
            {%- else -%}
              <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ v.id }}" data-lpdp-variant-id>
            {%- endif -%}

            <div class="lpdp__buy">
              {%- comment -%}
                Capped at three, not a 1 to 50 dropdown. Lada is cut in
                small numbers and a fifty-deep select on a made-to-measure
                cotton is a promise the workshop cannot keep.

                The plus and minus ship `hidden` and the script reveals
                them, so with the script blocked the customer gets a plain
                number field whose own min and max the browser enforces,
                rather than two buttons that do nothing. That is the same
                rule the size table's unit toggle follows.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <div class="lpdp__qty" data-lpdp-qty>
                <button class="lpdp__qty-b" type="button" data-lpdp-step="-1"
                        aria-label="One fewer" hidden>&minus;</button>
                <input class="lpdp__qty-i" type="number" inputmode="numeric"
                       name="quantity" value="1" min="1" max="{{ qty_max }}" step="1"
                       aria-label="{{ qty_label | escape }}">
                <button class="lpdp__qty-b" type="button" data-lpdp-step="1"
                        aria-label="One more" hidden>+</button>
              </div>

              <button class="lpdp__atc" type="submit" data-lpdp-atc
                      {% unless product.available %}disabled{% endunless %}>
                <span class="lpdp__atc-t" data-lpdp-atc-t>
                  {%- if product.available -%}{{ add_label }}{%- else -%}{{ sold_label }}{%- endif -%}
                </span>
                <span class="lpdp__spin" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              </button>
            </div>

            <p class="lpdp__err" data-kj-atc-error role="alert" hidden></p>
          </form>

          {%- comment -%}
            The description, exactly as the client typed it in Shopify. No
            heading over it, no accordion, nothing written around it. If it
            is empty the column simply ends at the button.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if product.description != blank -%}
            <div class="lpdp__desc rte">{{ product.description }}</div>
          {%- endif -%}

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  Behaviour for this section and nothing else. It lives here rather than
  in kj-v2.js because that file's PDP handlers bind .pdp__form and
  .pdp__buy and would fight this markup (see the note at the top), and
  because the section is the only thing on the site that needs it. It is
  written to no-op on every branch it does not find.

  IT RUNS MORE THAN ONCE, AND THAT IS THE THING TO GET RIGHT.

  In the theme editor every saved setting re-renders this section, and
  each render brings another copy of this script tag. The previous
  version guarded its per-section work on a flag and then, underneath
  that guard, added two listeners to the DOCUMENT for the size guide.
  The document is not re-rendered, so those two survived every reload
  and stacked up: a client nudging the size chart a dozen times left two
  dozen listeners behind, every one of them holding a <details> element
  that had been thrown away, and every one of them still running on
  every click and every keypress for the rest of the session.

  So the work is split by lifetime, which is the only honest way to
  divide it:

    PER SECTION (init)     everything bound to elements inside the root.
                           It dies with the root when the root is
                           replaced, so it only needs guarding against
                           running twice over the SAME root, which is
                           what data-lpdp-ready does.

    PER DOCUMENT (once)    the size guide's click-outside and Escape,
                           and the shopify:section:load re-init. Bound a
                           single time behind a flag on <html>, and they
                           look the open guide up when they fire instead
                           of closing over one, so they keep working
                           against a root that did not exist when they
                           were bound and hold nothing that can go stale.

  The re-init exists because a section replaced by innerHTML does not
  necessarily re-execute the script tags inside it. Both paths are safe:
  if the inline call below runs first, the event handler finds the flag
  already set and returns; if it does not run at all, the event handler
  is what sets this page up. That is also why the labels are read off
  the root's data- attributes rather than printed into this closure. The
  handler was bound by whichever copy of this script ran first, and that
  copy's idea of what the Add button says is exactly the stale value a
  client who has just renamed it is looking for.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script>
  (function () {
    'use strict';

    var DOC_FLAG = 'data-lpdp-doc';

    function openGuides() {
      return document.querySelectorAll('[data-lpdp-guide][open]');
    }

    function init(root) {
      if (!root || root.getAttribute('data-lpdp-ready')) return;
      root.setAttribute('data-lpdp-ready', 'true');

      var ADD  = root.getAttribute('data-lpdp-add')  || '';
      var SOLD = root.getAttribute('data-lpdp-sold') || '';
      var UNAV = root.getAttribute('data-lpdp-unav') || '';

      var atc     = root.querySelector('[data-lpdp-atc]');
      var label   = root.querySelector('[data-lpdp-atc-t]');
      var idField = root.querySelector('[data-lpdp-variant-id]');
      var prices  = root.querySelectorAll('[data-lpdp-vprice]');

      var map = [];
      var json = root.querySelector('[data-lpdp-variants]');
      if (json) { try { map = JSON.parse(json.textContent) || []; } catch (e) { map = []; } }

      function byId(id) {
        for (var i = 0; i < map.length; i++) {
          if (String(map[i].id) === String(id)) return map[i];
        }
        return null;
      }

      /* Everything a chosen variant changes, in one place. The price is
         never computed: the server rendered every variant's price into the
         markup and this only picks which one is not hidden. */
      function paint(variant) {
        if (!variant) {
          if (atc) atc.disabled = true;
          if (label) label.textContent = UNAV;
          return;
        }
        if (idField) idField.value = variant.id;
        if (atc) atc.disabled = !variant.available;
        if (label) label.textContent = variant.available ? ADD : SOLD;
        for (var i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {
          prices[i].hidden = prices[i].getAttribute('data-lpdp-vprice') !== String(variant.id);
        }
      }

      /* --- one option: the radio already is the variant id --- */
      var direct = root.querySelectorAll('[data-lpdp-variant-radio]');
      if (direct.length) {
        root.addEventListener('change', function (e) {
          var r = e.target.closest ? e.target.closest('[data-lpdp-variant-radio]') : null;
          if (r && r.checked) paint(byId(r.value));
        });
        var onNow = root.querySelector('[data-lpdp-variant-radio]:checked');
        if (onNow) paint(byId(onNow.value));
      } else {
        /* --- several options: match the chosen combination --- */
        var groups = root.querySelectorAll('[data-lpdp-group]');
        if (groups.length && idField) {
          var sync = function () {
            var chosen = [];
            for (var g = 0; g < groups.length; g++) {
              var on = groups[g].querySelector('input:checked');
              chosen.push(on ? on.value : null);
            }
            var match = null;
            for (var i = 0; i < map.length && !match; i++) {
              var ok = true;
              for (var k = 0; k < chosen.length; k++) {
                if (map[i].options[k] !== chosen[k]) ok = false;
              }
              if (ok) match = map[i];
            }
            paint(match);
          };
          root.addEventListener('change', function (e) {
            if (e.target.closest && e.target.closest('[data-lpdp-group]')) sync();
          });
          sync();
        }
      }

      /* --- quantity, capped --- */
      var qwrap = root.querySelector('[data-lpdp-qty]');
      if (qwrap) {
        var input = qwrap.querySelector('input');
        var steps = qwrap.querySelectorAll('[data-lpdp-step]');
        var max = parseInt(input.getAttribute('max'), 10) || 3;

        var sweep = function () {
          var n = parseInt(input.value, 10) || 1;
          for (var i = 0; i < steps.length; i++) {
            var d = parseInt(steps[i].getAttribute('data-lpdp-step'), 10);
            steps[i].disabled = d > 0 ? n >= max : n <= 1;
          }
        };

        for (var st = 0; st < steps.length; st++) {
          steps[st].hidden = false;
          steps[st].addEventListener('click', function (e) {
            var by = parseInt(e.currentTarget.getAttribute('data-lpdp-step'), 10) || 0;
            var n = (parseInt(input.value, 10) || 1) + by;
            input.value = Math.min(max, Math.max(1, n));
            sweep();
          });
        }

        input.addEventListener('change', function () {
          var n = parseInt(input.value, 10);
          if (isNaN(n) || n < 1) n = 1;
          if (n > max) n = max;
          input.value = n;
          sweep();
        });

        qwrap.setAttribute('data-ready', 'true');
        sweep();
      }
    }

    init(document.getElementById('lpdp-{{ section.id }}'));

    /* --- the three things that belong to the document, bound once --- */
    if (!document.documentElement.hasAttribute(DOC_FLAG)) {
      document.documentElement.setAttribute(DOC_FLAG, '');

      document.addEventListener('shopify:section:load', function (e) {
        var t = e.target;
        if (!t || !t.querySelector) return;
        init(t.matches && t.matches('[data-lpdp]') ? t : t.querySelector('[data-lpdp]'));
      });

      /* The size guide closes the way a popover is expected to. <details>
         gives the open and the close for free and works with this script
         blocked; these two are the only things it does not give. */
      document.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
        var open = openGuides();
        for (var i = 0; i < open.length; i++) {
          if (!open[i].contains(e.target)) open[i].open = false;
        }
      });

      document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
        if (e.key !== 'Escape') return;
        var open = openGuides();
        if (!open.length) return;
        open[0].open = false;
        var sm = open[0].querySelector('summary');
        if (sm) sm.focus();
      });
    }
  })();
</script>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Lada product",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "header", "content": "Buttons" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "add_label", "label": "Add button", "default": "Add to bag" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "sold_label", "label": "Sold out", "default": "Sold out" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "unav_label", "label": "Combination not made", "default": "Unavailable" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "qty_label", "label": "Quantity field label", "default": "Quantity",
      "info": "Read out by screen readers. Not printed on the page." },
    { "type": "range", "id": "qty_max", "label": "Most one customer can add",
      "min": 1, "max": 10, "step": 1, "default": 3 },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Colour and size" },
    { "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "How a product knows which kind it is. An option named in the first box becomes a colour choice with swatches and the measuring line. An option named in the second becomes size buttons with the size guide beside them. Nothing here is tied to a collection, so a new product works the day it is created." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "colour_options", "label": "Colour option names", "default": "colour,color",
      "info": "Comma separated. Capitals do not matter." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "size_options", "label": "Size option names", "default": "size",
      "info": "Comma separated. Capitals do not matter." },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Measured in the shop" },
    { "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "One line under the colour choice, for Alamgeer, the 100% combed cotton. Clear a box to hide that half." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "measure_line", "label": "The line",
      "default": "Cut to your measurement in the shop, free." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "measure_cta", "label": "WhatsApp button",
      "default": "Send your measurements" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "whatsapp", "label": "WhatsApp number", "default": "923019090590",
      "info": "Country code, no plus sign and no spaces. Leave blank to hide the button." },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "whatsapp_text", "label": "Pre filled message",
      "default": "Hello Khan Jee Center. I would like to be measured for",
      "info": "The product name and the page address are added to the end automatically, so end this on a word the name can follow." },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Size guide" },
    { "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Opens beside the size buttons. Leave the chart boxes empty to use the standard waistcoat chart." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "guide_label", "label": "Link text", "default": "Size guide" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "size_columns", "label": "Column headings",
      "info": "Comma separated. First one is the size column." },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "size_rows", "label": "Rows",
      "info": "Separate rows with a vertical bar and cells with commas. Inches, as plain numbers." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "size_note", "label": "Note under the chart" }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Lada product" }]
}
{% endschema %}
